
(verb
  :name "make-parameter" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "constructor"
  :signature "(make-parameter init) or (make-parameter init converter) -> procedure"
  :summary "R7RS §4.2.6 — build a parameter object: a procedure of no arguments that returns a value `parameterize` can rebind for the duration of a body."
  :explanation "A parameter is a setting that a whole region of code can read without anybody having to pass it down. You make one with an initial value, you READ it by calling it with no arguments, and you change it only with `parameterize`, which installs a new value for the dynamic extent of its body and puts the old one back on the way out — on every way out, including an escape and a raise. That last part is the reason parameters exist rather than a plain `define` plus `set!`: a global you set! by hand stays set when the body blows up, and a parameter does not. The optional second argument is a CONVERTER, a procedure of one argument run over every value the parameter is ever given. It runs on the initial value too, which surprises people: (make-parameter 5 (lambda (x) (* x 2))) reads back 10, not 5. Use the converter for validation and normalisation — coerce to a symbol, clamp to a range, reject nonsense with a default — and it will hold for the init value and for every parameterize alike, which is exactly the invariant you wanted. What a parameter is NOT is a variable: calling it with an argument does not set it. In Motoi that call is silently ignored and you get the current value back, so a mistaken (p 9) looks like it worked and did nothing."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(procedure? (make-parameter 42))" :note "#t — a parameter IS a procedure; you read it by calling it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(let ((tempo (make-parameter 120))) (tempo))" :note "120 — calling it with no arguments gives the current value, which starts out as init.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((tempo (make-parameter 120))) (list (tempo) (parameterize ((tempo 90)) (tempo)) (tempo)))" :note "(120 90 120) — parameterize rebinds for its body only, and the old value comes back after.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((size (make-parameter 5 (lambda (x) (* x 2))))) (list (size) (parameterize ((size 7)) (size)) (size)))" :note "(10 14 10) — the converter runs on the INITIAL value as well, so 5 reads back as 10.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((p (make-parameter 1))) (list (p 9) (p)))" :note "(1 1) — passing an argument does NOT set the parameter; the 9 is discarded without complaint.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define depth (make-parameter 0))\n(define (nest thunk)\n  (parameterize ((depth (+ 1 (depth)))) (thunk)))\n(display (list (depth) (nest (lambda () (list (depth) (nest (lambda () (depth)))))) (depth)))\n(newline)" :note "(0 (1 2) 0) — nesting parameterize stacks the rebindings and unwinds them in order.")
  )
  :caveats ("The converter is applied to the INIT value too, not only to values supplied by parameterize. (make-parameter 5 (lambda (x) (* x 2))) reads back 10." "Calling a parameter with an argument is silently ignored in Motoi: (p 9) returns the current value and does not change it. R7RS leaves this unspecified; other Schemes raise. Do not write it expecting a set." "The only supported way to change a parameter is parameterize. There is no parameter-set!." "The rebinding is undone on EVERY exit from the body — normal return, a call/cc escape, and a raise caught further out all restore the old value." "A handler installed outside a parameterize sees the OUTER value: (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (p)) (lambda () (parameterize ((p 'inner)) (error \"boom\")))) reports outer, because the unwind happens before the handler runs." "Without a converter any value is accepted, including a list or a procedure; validation is entirely the converter's job.")
  :usecases ("A rendering setting — indent width, colour depth, number base — that a whole subtree of calls should read without being handed it." "Swapping a real dependency for a test double over one region: a clock, a random source, an output sink." "A recursion depth or nesting level that increments on the way in and restores itself on the way out." "Normalising or clamping a setting once, in the converter, so no reader has to re-check it.")
  :related ("parameterize" "dynamic-wind" "call-with-current-continuation" "with-exception-handler" "define" "set!")
  :learn (:concept "A parameter is a value scoped to a stretch of TIME rather than to a stretch of text — every call made during the body sees it, and nothing after the body does." :prerequisites ("lambda" "let" "procedure?") :progression "Make one and read it. Wrap a parameterize around the read and watch the value change and change back. Then add a converter and notice it fired on the init value. Then nest two parameterize forms and read the depth at each level.")
  :provenance "R7RS §4.2.6; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1511 (Parameter + paramReader); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define volume (make-parameter 5))\n(define (render tag) (list tag (volume)))\n(display (list (render 'normal) (parameterize ((volume 0)) (render 'muted)) (render 'normal-again)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (make-parameter 'a))\n(display (list (p) (parameterize ((p 'b)) (parameterize ((p 'c)) (p))) (p)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define handle (make-parameter 'default-sink))\n(define (emit x) (list (handle) x))\n(display (parameterize ((handle 'test-sink)) (emit 1)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A deeply nested drawing routine needs to know the current volume, or base, or output sink, and the routines between the caller and the reader have no business knowing about it. Candidates: thread the setting through every intermediate procedure as an extra argument (explicit, and it changes the signature of every function on the path), use a global plus set! and set it back afterwards (two lines, and it stays wrong if the body raises), or make it a parameter."
     :explanation "eng: render never takes a volume argument and still sees the muted value, and only during that one call. math: the inner rebinding wins while it is in scope and the outer one is restored exactly, so the sequence is a b→c then back to a. cs: the test double is installed for one expression and nothing else in the program can observe it."
     :meta "Reach for make-parameter and NOT a global with set! when the change is scoped to a body. The set! version is correct only on the happy path — the moment the body raises, the global keeps the temporary value and every later reader is wrong, with no error to point at. Reach for a plain argument instead when only one or two procedures need it: a parameter hides the dependency, and hiding it is worth it only when the path is long.")
    (:eng "The intermediate procedures stay ignorant of the setting, which is the whole point — they did not need to know." :math "Nested rebinding is a stack: innermost wins, and unwinding restores exactly the value that was shadowed." :cs "This is dependency injection with dynamic scope: swap the collaborator for the extent of a call rather than at construction."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "One value that a whole region of work agrees on, installed at the top of the region and gone at the bottom."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display (let ((tempo (make-parameter 120))) (list (tempo) (parameterize ((tempo 90)) (tempo)))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the unit system a whole worksheet is written in" :code "(define unit (make-parameter 'metric))\n(display (list (unit) (parameterize ((unit 'imperial)) (unit)) (unit)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the base a numeral is written in" :code "(define base (make-parameter 10))\n(define (digits n) (list n 'base (base)))\n(display (list (digits 255) (parameterize ((base 16)) (digits 255))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A worksheet declares its units once at the top and every number on the page is read in them; a numeral means nothing until you know its base. In both cases the value is not part of the thing being written — 255 is 255 — it is part of the CONTEXT that says how to read it. A parameter is that declaration made executable: it does not change what the inner code says, it changes what the inner code means."
     :meta "Everything on this ladder shares one hazard: the context is invisible at the point of use. `(digits 255)` looks self-contained and is not, and that is the price of not threading the argument. Pay it when the path is long enough that threading would be worse.")
    (:eng "A setting declared once at the top of a region is a contract every reader in the region inherits." :math "The value is a parameter of the interpretation, not of the expression; the expression is the same in both readings." :cs "Dynamic scope: the binding is found by who is CALLING, not by where the code is written."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define p (make-parameter 1))\n(display (list 'outside (p) 'inside (parameterize ((p 2)) (p)) 'restored (p)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (make-parameter 5 (lambda (x) (* x 2))))\n(display (list (p) (parameterize ((p 7)) (p)) (p)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define p (make-parameter 'orig))\n(display (list 'ignored-write (p 'attempted-set) 'still (p)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "parameterize rebinds for its body and restores exactly on exit; the converter, if given, is applied to the init value as well as to every rebinding; and calling the parameter with an argument changes nothing."
     :invariant "For a parameter p with converter c: p() = c(init) before and after any parameterize, and p() = c(v) inside (parameterize ((p v)) …); and for any x, evaluating (p x) leaves p() unchanged"
     :meta "The second and third programs are the ones that carry information. (10 14 10) is the converter proving it fired on init — a reader who assumed the converter only touches parameterize values would predict (5 14 5) and be wrong. The third is the unflattering one: (p 'attempted-set) answers `orig` and does nothing, so a typo that meant to be a set is invisible. What would falsify the claim: a value that survived past the closing paren of parameterize, or a converter that skipped the init value.")
    (:eng "Restored-to-1 after the body is the property that makes this safe to use in a place that can fail." :math "c(init) rather than init is the exact statement people get wrong; write the converter so c(c(x)) = c(x) and it stops mattering." :cs "A silent no-op write is worse than an error; treat (p x) as a bug in your code, not as an API."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define indent (make-parameter 0))\n(define (line s) (list (indent) s))\n(define (deeper thunk) (parameterize ((indent (+ 1 (indent)))) (thunk)))\n(display (list (line 'a) (deeper (lambda () (list (line 'b) (deeper (lambda () (line 'c))))))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define scale (make-parameter 1))\n(define (measure x) (* x (scale)))\n(display (map (lambda (s) (parameterize ((scale s)) (measure 10))) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define clock (make-parameter (lambda () 'real)))\n(define (stamp e) (list ((clock)) e))\n(display (list (stamp 'a) (parameterize ((clock (lambda () 'frozen))) (stamp 'b))))\n(newline)"
     :base "make-parameter gives a value that parameterize can rebind for the extent of a body."
     :emergent "eng: reading the parameter to compute its own next value turns it into a DEPTH counter that unwinds itself — no decrement anywhere, because the restore is the decrement. math: parameterizing inside a map sweeps the setting over a list and collects one answer per setting, turning a context into an axis. cs: the parameter holds a PROCEDURE, so the thing being swapped is behaviour rather than data, and the test double is installed for one expression."
     :meta "The composition that generates all three is (parameterize ((p (f (p)))) …) — read the current value, derive the new one, and let the unwind undo it. Once you see that, a parameter stops being a setting and becomes a small stack you never have to pop. The third is the one to keep for testing: a parameter holding a lambda is the cheapest seam in the language.")
    (:eng "The restore IS the decrement — a counter with no decrement site cannot drift." :math "Sweeping the parameter over a list turns one context into a family of results indexed by it." :cs "A parameter holding a procedure is dependency injection; a parameter holding data is configuration. Same verb, different leverage."))
)

(verb
  :name "emergency-exit" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "effect"
  :signature "(emergency-exit) or (emergency-exit obj) -> does not return"
  :summary "R7RS §6.14 — stop the program right now, running no cleanup of any kind."
  :explanation "The blunt instrument. It terminates the running program at the point of call: nothing after it evaluates, no dynamic-wind after-thunk fires, no exception handler sees anything, and no error is reported. Where `raise` says 'something went wrong, somebody deal with it' and an escape says 'leave this form', emergency-exit says 'the process is over'. That is not a defect — it is what R7RS defines emergency-exit to be, and it is precisely how it differs from the ordinary `exit`, which is supposed to unwind first. Motoi does not bind `exit` at all, so emergency-exit is the only termination verb here and there is no polite variant to reach for instead. The argument, if you pass one, becomes the process exit status: no argument or 0 means success, a positive integer becomes that status. Be careful with booleans. R7RS says #t means success and #f means failure; Motoi computes the status arithmetically from the value, which makes (emergency-exit #t) exit with status 1 and (emergency-exit #f) exit with status 0 — exactly backwards from the standard. Pass integers and the ambiguity disappears. Because it is `'destructive'`-tiered and because it defeats every cleanup mechanism in the language, this belongs at the top level of a program that is genuinely finished, not inside a library that someone else will call."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(procedure? emergency-exit)" :note "#t — it is an ordinary procedure, not syntax; it just never comes back.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(begin (display \"work done\") (newline) (emergency-exit 0) (display \"never printed\") (newline))" :note "prints `work done` and stops; the second display is dead code the moment emergency-exit is reached.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(begin (display \"before\") (newline) (emergency-exit) (display \"after\") (newline))" :note "prints `before` only. With no argument the exit status is 0, the same as (emergency-exit 0).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[in]\")) (lambda () (emergency-exit 0)) (lambda () (display \"[out]\")))" :note "prints `[in]` and nothing else — the after-thunk does NOT run. This is the whole point of the verb.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(begin (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (display \"handler ran\")) (lambda () (emergency-exit 0))) (display \"after\") (newline))" :note "prints nothing at all. Terminating is not raising, so no handler is consulted.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (checked-run thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler\n    (lambda (e) (display (list 'failing (error-object-message e))) (newline) (emergency-exit 0))\n    thunk))\n(checked-run (lambda () (error \"tile out of range\" 99)))\n(display \"not reached\")\n(newline)" :note "prints `(failing tile out of range)` and stops — report first, then terminate, because after emergency-exit there is no reporting.")
  )
  :caveats ("dynamic-wind after-thunks do NOT run. Any cleanup you were relying on is skipped; that is the definition of the verb, not a bug." "with-exception-handler cannot catch it. It is termination, not a raise." "R7RS's boolean convention is INVERTED here: (emergency-exit #t) exits with status 1 and (emergency-exit #f) exits with status 0, where the standard says #t is success and #f is failure. Pass integers instead." "Motoi does not bind `exit` — (procedure? exit) reports `unbound symbol: exit`. R7RS §6.14 specifies both; only the emergency form exists." "It is tagged 'destructive' in the permission registry, alongside the other verbs that can end or damage a session." "Never call it from a library or a helper someone else composes with; it removes their ability to recover, and they have no way to intercept it.")
  :usecases ("Ending a top-level script that has finished its work and has nothing to unwind." "Stopping immediately after reporting a fatal condition, when continuing would produce wrong output." "Setting a nonzero exit status so a surrounding shell or build step can see that the run failed." "Aborting a batch the moment an unrecoverable input is seen, in a program whose output is already flushed.")
  :related ("with-exception-handler" "dynamic-wind" "raise" "error" "call-with-current-continuation" "command-line")
  :learn (:concept "There is a difference between leaving a computation and ending the process, and only one of them gives anybody else a chance to react." :prerequisites ("display" "dynamic-wind" "with-exception-handler") :progression "Put it after a display and see the next line never print. Wrap it in a dynamic-wind and notice the after-thunk is skipped. Wrap it in a handler and notice the handler is skipped too. Then decide, every time you reach for it, whether you actually wanted raise.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.14; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1436 (process.exit(code|0)), permission tier 'destructive'; measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(display \"saving\")\n(newline)\n(emergency-exit 0)\n(display \"this line is dead code\")\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define n 0)\n(set! n 1)\n(display n)\n(newline)\n(emergency-exit 0)\n(set! n 2)\n(display n)\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[acquire]\")) (lambda () (display \"[body]\") (emergency-exit 0)) (lambda () (display \"[release]\")))"
     :problem "A script has hit a condition it cannot continue past. Candidates: raise an error and let it propagate (the caller can catch and recover, and cleanup runs — but in a top-level script with no caller the message goes out as a crash), return a sentinel and hope every layer above checks it (they will not), or terminate immediately with emergency-exit."
     :explanation "eng: the line after the call is unreachable, and unreachable in the strong sense — not skipped, not conditional, never evaluated. math: the second set! never happens, so the last observable state of n is 1. cs: [acquire] and [body] print and [release] does not, which is the cost of the third candidate stated plainly."
     :meta "Reach for emergency-exit and NOT raise only when you are the top level and there is genuinely nobody to inform. Anywhere else, raise: it carries a message, it runs cleanup, and a caller can decide. The [acquire] with no [release] is the whole argument — if the acquire had been a real resource, you just leaked it deliberately.")
    (:eng "Dead code after a terminator is worth deleting; leaving it there tells the next reader something false about the flow." :math "The observable state of the program is a prefix of the state sequence it would otherwise have had." :cs "Same as _exit versus exit in C: one runs the atexit handlers and one does not, and knowing which you called matters."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A stop that is not a pause and not a return — the machine is off, and nothing downstream of it observes anything."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display \"done\")\n(newline)\n(emergency-exit 0)")
       (:domain "pulling the power on a running machine rather than shutting it down" :code "(define (abort-now) (emergency-exit 0))\n(display \"pulling the plug\")\n(newline)\n(abort-now)\n(display \"nope\")\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the last term of a sequence that has no successor" :code "(display (list 'last-thing-observed 1))\n(newline)\n(emergency-exit 0)\n(display (list 'never 2))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "Shutting a machine down and pulling its power both stop it, and only one of them lets it put its tools away. A sequence that terminates has a last term, and asking what comes after the last term is not a hard question — it is a question with no referent. emergency-exit makes the rest of the program into exactly that: not skipped code, not unreachable-by-condition code, but code with no time in which to happen."
     :meta "The ladder's common shape is that the STOPPING is cheap and the CLEANING UP is what you gave up. Every rung has a polite version that costs more and leaves the world tidy, and choosing the rude one is a decision about whether the world needs to be tidy afterwards.")
    (:eng "The difference between shutdown and power-loss is entirely about what got written before the lights went out." :math "A terminating sequence has no term after its last; 'what happens next' is undefined, not empty." :cs "Ungraceful termination is a legitimate tool with one legitimate location: the outermost frame."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display \"a\")\n(emergency-exit 0)\n(display \"b\")"
     :math-program "(display (list 1 2 3))\n(newline)\n(emergency-exit 0)\n(display (list 4 5 6))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (display \"caught\") (newline)) (lambda () (emergency-exit 0)))\n(display \"resumed\")\n(newline)"
     :claim "Nothing textually after an evaluated emergency-exit produces any observable effect, and no cleanup or handling mechanism in the language intercepts it: not dynamic-wind's after-thunk, not with-exception-handler."
     :invariant "For any program P = A ++ [(emergency-exit n)] ++ B where A terminates normally, output(P) = output(A) and the process status is n"
     :meta "The third program is the sharpest: it prints NOTHING — not `caught`, not `resumed`. A reader who models emergency-exit as 'a raise that nobody catches' would predict `caught`, and would be wrong, because it is not a raise at all. What would falsify the claim: any output from B, or a handler or after-thunk firing. The one place the claim is deliberately narrow is the status: with an integer it is exact, with a boolean Motoi inverts the standard's convention, which is why the examples pass integers.")
    (:eng "output(P) = output(A) is the entire specification and it is testable in one line." :math "The program is truncated at the call, and truncation is not the same as the tail being empty." :cs "Not catchable, not windable: it sits outside the condition system rather than at the top of it."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (die-if bad msg)\n  (if bad (begin (display msg) (newline) (emergency-exit 0))))\n(die-if #f \"not this\")\n(display \"still alive\")\n(newline)\n(die-if #t \"fatal: cart is corrupt\")\n(display \"unreachable\")\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (run-all steps)\n  (for-each (lambda (s) (if (eq? s 'stop) (emergency-exit 0) (begin (display s) (display \" \")))) steps))\n(run-all (list 'a 'b 'stop 'c))\n(newline)\n(display \"never\")\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (guarded thunk)\n  (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[open]\")) thunk (lambda () (display \"[close]\"))))\n(guarded (lambda () (display \"ok\")))\n(newline)\n(guarded (lambda () (emergency-exit 0)))\n(display \"never\")\n(newline)"
     :base "emergency-exit ends the process at the point of call, running no cleanup."
     :emergent "eng: wrapped in a conditional it becomes an ASSERTION — a guard that costs one line at each check site and needs no propagation anywhere. math: inside a for-each it terminates the whole program rather than the loop, which is a much bigger hammer than the escape you probably wanted. cs: paired with dynamic-wind it demonstrates the failure mode directly — [open]ok[close] on the good run, and bare [open] on the run that exits, with the close missing."
     :meta "The composition worth keeping is the first and the composition worth fearing is the third. A die-if guard at the top level of a script is honest and cheap. But the moment emergency-exit is buried inside a helper that a dynamic-wind wraps, you have written a resource leak that no amount of correct code around it can repair — the [open] with no [close] is right there in the output. If you want to stop a LOOP, use call-with-current-continuation; if you want to stop a COMPUTATION, use raise; emergency-exit stops the world, and nothing smaller."
    )
    (:eng "An assertion that terminates is only acceptable where terminating is acceptable — that is, at the top." :math "Exiting from inside an iteration exits the program, not the iteration; the scope of the effect is the process." :cs "[open] without [close] is the leak made visible; treat it as the reason not to put this verb in a library."))
)
